\f1\fs22 Tony put the finishing touches on a peanut butter, banana and bacon sandwich; he was looking out the kitchen window over the back gardens, about to take the first bite when a sound as terrifying as a jet engine falling on a gigantic drum kit assaulted him from behind. Still holding the sandwich inches from his lips, he turned in mute shock as the mansion collapsed like a little pig\'92s house made of sticks and golden toilet bowls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was regarding his sandwich, considering whether he could take it with him, when a ghostly plume of dust whooshed from the wreckage and layered his beloved meal with grit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He ran outside, around the largely still standing back corner of the mansion towards the front, fearing the parlour room would be rubble. He was sure Sarah was in there, and now was not the time to lose her. He stopped running when he saw the degree of damage: there wasn\'92t any. It was a miracle. He almost wept. From where he stood, not far from the parlour room verandah, it appeared as though the mansion was as new as the day it was painted. As he gaped in amazement, his fingers and thumb pressed deep into the wonder-white, soft bread of his sandwich.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise was the only occupant of the mansion who didn\'92t shield her head under her hands, or look for a piece of furniture to dive under, or a window to dive out. Instead, she grabbed her tablet and went to work. If they\'92d survived beyond the first few seconds, then they\'92d probably survive the strike, at least until the next order was given. Although she\'92d never been in a field of battle, she knew how surgically short military conflict was. All modern weapons were guided by electronics and software; once the trigger was pulled, they never missed. As Eloise calmly scrolled and tapped, she scoffed at those sci-fi adventure tropes where the evil soldiers couldn\'92t shoot for shit and only the token sidekick got hit for the sake of a modicum of pathos. For all the pyrotechnics of laser beams continually missing their targets, the typical space opera was no more than a futuristic cosplay spaghetti western. Minus the special effects, the weapons were no more sophisticated or accurate than arrows and nineteenth-century rifles.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Based on the telemetry from the offensive hardware surrounding the mansion, Eloise watched a simulation of the strike. A PAPER gunship\'92s particle gun blasted the mansion; a tank blasted the gunship; a second gunship blasted the tank. The whole thing lasted less than eight-tenths of a second before software called a ceasefire and awaited further orders.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Those orders came from Eloise. Assessing PAPER had believed their targets were in the other front room, and probably still thought they\'92d been neutralised, Eloise grinned: yet another case of a failure of intelligence rather than weaponry. Which gave Eloise the edge. After passing the usual authentication protocols, she accessed the firmware backdoors of all the assets in play, compiled a list and gave a theatre-wide command to shut down. The gunships landed, regardless of what their pilots countermanded, and went silent. The tanks froze, unresponsive. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise had done the unthinkable, exposing her capability to disable the weaponry of both sides. A one-time play, she\'92d never sell armaments again.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax it
\f1\i0 , she thought. 
\f2\i I\'92m tired of selling boys\'92 toys to toy boys anyway
\f1\i0 . She\'92d never rule the world. She snorted a laugh at the delusional grandiosity of the ambition, and realised; way down in the innocent, tender places of her heart, she always hoped she might one day.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91C\'92mon,\'92 Eloise told the three other women. \'91We\'92re leaving.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley assumed the role of officer-in-charge, opened the door to the hallway. A small avalanche of refuse rolled, tilted and wafted into the parlour. Hayley jumped back and coughed as if her life was dependent on it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise found the french doors to the patio we locked. Kathy launched towards a window and squealed with fright as a creature-like shadow leapt onto the lace curtain. The creature heaved the window up and stuck its threatening head in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Mother!\'92 the creature cried. \'91Thank fortunes, you\'92re OK.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise wheeled on Alexander. \'91What the tax happened? Did you poke those PAPER agents?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still standing outside the window with his upper half poking through and the curtain, looking like an over-size sock puppet with a dishevelled bridal veil over his head, Alexander said, \'91No mother, and if I find out which idiot assistant producer put this scene on the call sheet\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Get out of the way!\'92 Kathy ordered Alexander. \'91Expecting mothers first.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fuggle Chops!\'92 Alexander reached through the window.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Wait!\'92 Eloise ordered. \'91We can\'92t expose Sarah to PAPER eyes yet.\'92 Eloise wouldn\'92t risk going out there. Yet. She produced her tablet and called up a device sniffer, which listed everything within two kilometres of the nearest comms tower, even the clandestine ones.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Agent Jones is running the PAPER operation,\'92 Hayley told Eloise. \'91He\'92s been chasing Sarah since\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise hushed Hayley by raising her finger. 
\f2\i Wait
\f1\i0 . \'91Agent Jones I presume?\'92 Eloise said into her tablet. \'91Very good. I just want to\'97no, no, Agent Jones, you\'92ll have to save that for another day \'85 unless you want all your fancy machinery in the immediate area to explode. No? Good \'85 Presently, I\'92ll be leaving the premises in my limousine with my son and three of my dearest friends. I expect\'97no, Agent Jones, I will do nothing of the kind, and I suggest you learn some manners. If you want to rant at me, please keep it until after I have shared some very important information, am I making myself clear? Good, I\'92ll take the sound of you grinding your teeth as an affirmation. Now, if you\'92re wondering how your gunships were disabled, I took such action in accordance with the Warranties and Service, and the Proprietary Rights provisions of our contract. Well, if John Deere can stop a farmer from fixing his own tractor, I can stop jarheads from blowing shit up, especially if the jarheads blow shit up around me. \'85 Yes, I understand your position \'85 I 
\f2\i understand
\f1\i0  your position, but you have violated the terms I have with your masters who are so far above your pay grade, your salary is little more than pocket money from a lemonade stand. Yes, well I don\'92t care for lemonade either, but the point is, my companions and I 
\f2\i will 
\f1\i0 be leaving safely, because I have initiated a destruct sequence on all your hardware\'97all your gunships and all the particle pistols in your holsters, and everything in between. So unless you want a bucket-size hole through your pelvis where your banger and spuds used to be, I suggest you give me every affordance to leave here in peace, after which I shall cancel the detonation sequence. You have ten minutes from about \'85 a minute and a half ago. Do we have an understanding? Ah, you\'92re grinding your teeth again. Excellent. Good-bye, Agent Jones.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise dropped her tablet into her purse. \'91Sarah, you first.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander tried assisting Kathy with guiding Sarah over the window sill, but proved to be more of an impediment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let me do it,\'92 Sarah ordered, having quite enough of being treated an invalid.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise notes the ease with which Sarah vaulted out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You next,\'92 Kathy told Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley gave one shake of her head. \'91Civilians before police.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy nodded and leapt out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise kicked her shoes off. Hayley couldn\'92t help but rue the several months of her salary Eloise was so casually discarding. \'91Warlords before police too?\'92 Eloise asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Be my guest.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise exited in an elegant manner befitting her age. By comparison, Hayley felt like a manatee as she threw a leg over the windowsill.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony watched mutely dumb as Sarah emerged. His joy surged until it was quickly doused by dismay as Alexander and fawned over her. He staggered as he willed himself to go to Sarah, but felt like an impostor when Alexander was already saving her. If Sarah would just look his way, give him the cue he needed and asked for his help, he\'92d instantly transform into the hero he wished she would call for.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy, Alexander\'92s mother, and the detective jumped from the window. Just like that, Sarah was surrounded by people she\'92d rather be with. Eloise led them to her limousine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A barricade of Elvi quickly formed between them and the ground car. One swung a guitar off her hip and struck a chord which sounded thin and disharmonious in the open air. The others filled their lungs with breath to break forth with song.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They never sang a note. Eloise strode up to the guitarist, stripped her of her instrument, and bashed it to smithereens on her limousine\'92s hood. She slung the wreckage away in a high arc. It looked like a grotesque cat o\'92 six tails as it flailed and flew through the air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi rounded on Eloise who dusted her hands off in such a measured, mannered way, everyone knew, even after a the physical display, not a detail of her make-up required touching up. She stood her ground and gave the Elvi a quiet, sharp sermon. Tony watched them recoil. Eloise had clearly threatened them, probably about the tanks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander bundled himself into the front passenger seat of the limo; Eloise ushered the others into the back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony\'92s heart broke as Eloise\'92s cavalcade proceeded down the driveway at a royally dignified pace, past the inert tanks, through the gate, and between the silent gunships. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hey, Great Teacher,\'92 one of the Elvi called. \'91Great Teacher?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Huh?\'92 It was the first word Tony had uttered since the mansion had collapsed, and it caught in the cake of dust in his throat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you gonna to eat that sandwich?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise met the gazes of the three women sharing her limousine, and read the mix of relief, gratitude, and steely resoluteness in the faces. 
\f2\i I could buy each of you fifty times over
\f1\i0 , she thought, as she had bought countless dedicated and intelligent people. Cruel and blunt people too, and yet \'85 Eloise doubted any of these women could be bought. Sure, after all, she may never rule the world, but Eloise harboured an ambitious pleasure that, with these three women, her odds had improved.\

